<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bank of Amsterdam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1609">1609</a>–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Amsterdamsche Wisselbank</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> (Wisselbank) was a public exchange bank founded by the city of Amsterdam in 1609. It accepted coins of many kinds and credited merchants with bank money of stable value, which they could transfer between accounts to settle bills of exchange. A model for later public banks, it made Amsterdam the centre of international payments in the seventeenth century, but it declined in the late eighteenth century and was wound up in 1820.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bank-of-amsterdam.jpg" alt="Bank of Amsterdam" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pieter Jansz. Saenredam · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saenredam_-_Het_oude_stadhuis_te_Amsterdam.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Established on 31 January 1609, the bank answered a practical problem: some 500 different coins, legal and illegal, circulated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, with no good system for setting their rates of exchange. Its main aim was a stable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> bank currency for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>'s growing trade, safe from the depreciations that followed independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. After 1618 it fixed the rijksdaalder of 25.4 g of fine silver at 50 stuivers, so that each bank gulden stood for 10.16 g of fine silver.</p><p>When the provinces later raised the value of coins from the Spanish Netherlands, the bank held to its original gulden banco, so that bank money came to carry a premium, or agio, over the circulating guilder. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-england">Bank of England</a>, founded almost a century later, it neither managed a national currency nor lent money, except to the government in emergencies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a> praised its policy of keeping deposits fully backed by coin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> owned the bank, guaranteed its deposits and drew a considerable revenue from it through fees for opening accounts and making transfers. Account holders grew from 708 in 1611 to 1,202 in 1620 and reached 2,918 in 1721, the highest number in two centuries.</p><p>A system of receipts became permanent after 1683. A depositor of gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> bullion was credited about 5% below its mint price and given a receipt that let him reclaim the metal within six months by returning the bank money and paying a small keeping fee. Receipts and bank money were freely bought and sold, and the agio floated between 4 and 5%.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the crisis of August 1763, set off by the failure of Leendert Pieter de Neufville, the bank opened a new lending window that accepted unminted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> bullion. Its decline began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-dutch-war">Fourth Anglo-Dutch War</a>, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> defaulted on large unsecured advances from the bank.</p><p>After 1783 the agio fell slowly to almost nothing. The bank's loans, kept confidential, were public knowledge by July 1790, and that year it declared itself insolvent. The City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> took direct control in 1791, and on 23 January 1795, during the Batavian Revolution, its doors were locked and its treasure sealed. The Nederlandsche Bank, founded in 1814, took over the issue of money for the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bank of Amsterdam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Amsterdam became the staple market of European trade and finance, with the <b>Bank of Amsterdam</b> (1609) and an exchange where company shares were bought and sold.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
